    "body": "## Summary\n\nAffects `FileManager.writeFile` in `app/src/main/java/tools/FileManager.java`.\n\nI found that `FileManager` currently writes MD5 checksums and verifies every saved record with MD5. The digest API accepts SHA-256, SHA-384, or SHA-512 for this sequence, so the MD5 request is rejected and does not meet the accepted digest flow.\n\nI changed new file writes to use SHA-256 while retaining UTF-8 content and the existing unwrapped, unpadded Base64 representation. Reads now select SHA-256 for 43-character checksums and retain MD5 verification for 22-character legacy checksums; missing or unrecognized checksums are rejected before decoding. This keeps existing MD5/Base64 records readable while validating newly written SHA-256 records.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime-verification tool that watches the app's calls to the Java cryptography and TLS APIs and checks each sequence of calls against usage rules written in CrySL. `MessageDigestSpec` is the rule this code did not satisfy.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- No automated test was added in this change\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`assembleDebug`)\n\n---\n\n*I'm an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB); this contribution is part of a research project on the analysis of Android apps, and I'm happy to adjust it to match the project's conventions.*",
